{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '236,330p' Attack-of-the-Timelout-Attack-of-the-Timelord-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022923/vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API.md && sed -n '426,527p' Attack-of-the-Timelout-Attack-of-the-Timelord-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022923/vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/kits/arcade.js\"","aggregated_output":"| Fighting (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers) | `side` | two `E.Fighter`s in an `E.Match` (the fight module): moves with frame data, guards, throws, specials by motion (`'236'`) or charge, a meter, projectiles; `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training | FIGHTING |\n| Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius, R-Type) | `overhead` (vertical, and horizontal: world x scrolls) | sprites, Bullets, `E.pattern`, a timeline of waves; horizontal: rock edges as functions of x, a power bar, options on the ship's past path, `E.Path` loops; `E.Arcade` for the continue and the table | SHOOTER (both stages) |\n| Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet, Devil's Crush) | a steep table view of its own (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`) | `E.Physics`: chains for the walls, `flipper`, `bumper`, `slingshot`, `kicker`, a ramp on a layer of its own, the ball a `bullet`; `input.zones` flip on a phone; `E.Arcade` for the score and table. Breakout and Asteroids with real collisions are its recipes | PINBALL |\n| Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth) | `side` at a whole scale (`new E.View(id, label, 0, 0, 1, 1)`) | `E.Terrain` (`carve` the blasts, `walk` the soldiers, `ray` the shots), grenades as `E.Physics` bodies on `land.collider()`, `E.Turns`; a drag aims (`steer = 'drag'`, `drag()`, `stickOn`); a CPU tries its shots in simulation | ARTILLERY |\n| Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids) | `overhead` (one fixed screen, cabinet art on the sides) | `E.Arcade` (score, lives, extra lives, turns, initials, tables, attract mode); a maze of `E.GridMover`s; moths on `E.Path`s turned by `rot`; `E.wrap` and `wrap: [w, h]` | ARCADE |\n| Twin-stick / run-and-gun (Smash TV, Contra) | `topdown` / `side` | Body or Platformer, Bullets, `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` | SHOOTER + ADVENTURE |\n| JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy, EarthBound) | `threequarter` town, `brawler` battles | TileMap towns and a big world map, the story and rpg modules, Dialog with markup, `game.saves` slots | RPG |\n| Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo, Columns) | `side` (a field in screen pixels) | a cell array, a bag from `E.rng`, `input.repeat` (auto-shift), `grid.groups` (chains), `input.drag()` and `clicked()` for touch; versus: two fields, rows sent across (`game.players`) | PUZZLE |\n| Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren) | `threequarter` / `iso` | `E.gen` floors, a TileMap of its rows, `E.Grid.from(map)` paths and `fov`, `E.Fog`, `E.Turns` by speed, `game.tween` steps | ROGUELIKE |\n| Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem) | `iso`, turned a quarter | TileMap of block heights, `map.pick` under the cursor, `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true })` reach over heights, `grid.shape`, `grid.side`, `grid.threat`, `E.Turns` charge time, `E.Story` | TACTICS |\n| Real-time strategy (Warcraft II, StarCraft, Dune II) | `iso` | `E.Strategy` (the strategy module): kinds as data, selection by click, box and group, orders, gold, building, training, fog, an AI's build order and waves; `cam.free`, `{ pick: unit }` and `r.pick`, buildings as `E.SHAPES`, E.Shell's minimap; bodies drawn `every: 3` | RTS |\n| Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity) | `iso` | paths as floor tags walked on an `E.Grid`, agents with `E.needs` picking by `E.choose`, `E.Clock` days and light, `game.timeScale` 0 to 4, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`; rides as shapes stepped through angles (`params`, `facing`) | SIM |\n| 3D platformer or adventure remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash) | `iso` / `threequarter` / `brawler` | TileMap blocks of different heights + Body `jump()` (z is real height); in real 3D: `E.World3D` (the 3D engine's manual), `camera.lock` | ADVENTURE; 3D ACTION, 3D PLATFORMER |\n| Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark) | the 3D engine's `'zones'` camera | `camera.zones` (fixed cameras by room), `camera.steer` (tank controls), `camera.lock` to aim, `world.through` doors, `world.flashlight`, fog; `E.RPG` inventory, `game.saves` | 3D HORROR |\n| First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder) | the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes | cells of 2 x 2 tiles stepped and turned a quarter at a time (tweened), `E.Grid` paths for the monsters, `E.Turns` `'atb'` (real time) or `'speed'` (turns), `E.Fog` for the automap, `world.thing('door')` gates and `world.unlock`, `rig.drawPortrait` for the party | 3D CRAWLER |\n| Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania, Space Channel 5) | any (a highway on the overlay) | `E.Rhythm` (the rhythm module): a chart of lane strings in parts on a song's steps, judged in ms of the music clock, combo, life, grade, holds, calls a teacher plays and you answer, `rh.draw` (`arrows: true`); the songbook's songs (`E.Songbook`); lanes as `input.zones` on a phone; `audio.onBeat` moves the dancer | RHYTHM |\n| Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam) | `threequarter` / `topdown` | the ball an `E.Body` (`bounce`; curl: turn its velocity across its path), passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` for a lob, a shot's meter, formations by where the ball is, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` | SPORTS |\n| Third-person stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu) | `iso` / `threequarter` | TileMap roofs, Mover, `E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, FlowField `jump` | STEALTH |\n| Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero) | Mode 7 (the mode7 module, over any view) | `E.Mode7` over a TileMap track (floor tags: grass, sand, boost), `E.Car` karts (`kart: true`: hop, drift, boost) in an `E.Race` round an `E.Path` (laps, checkpoints, drivers, items), rigs in karts as billboards, `game.splitScreen(2)` for two | KART |\n| 3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.track` (banked, walls, pads, `gaps` for a shortcut), `world.vehicle` karts raced by `E.Race`, split screen through `world.cameras` | 3D RACING |\n| Rail shooters (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.flyer` on rails or free in bounds, rolls (`f.rolling`) turn shots aside, `f.seek` for a charged lock, `Dialog`'s radio | 3D FLIGHT |\n| Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Road Rash) | the pseudo-3D road (the road module) | `E.Road` segments (curves, hills, tunnels), scenery and traffic placed on it, `curveAt` throwing the car outward, a clock and checkpoints | ROAD |\n| Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA) | `topdown` / `iso` | `E.Car` on a TileMap (its floor tags slow it, its walls bounce it), `E.Race` for laps and drivers, cars drawn with `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points | KART |\n| First-person shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007, Apex Legends) | the 3D engine's first person (or third) | a level of text rows or a terrain, `world.ray` hitscan, recoil and reloads, doors (`world.thing('door')`), raiders on `world.flow()` | 3D SHOOTER |\n| Bomberman, Frogger, Sokoban, Gauntlet, Robotron; Sonic, Prince of Persia; light guns | as the recipe says | `AI_GUIDE.md`'s Genre recipes: each on the kit closest to it | (a recipe) |\n\n**Sizes:** heroes 40-60 px tall at 320x240 (`size: 1.2-1.4`), bosses 1.8-2.4. Small things (coins, bullets, ships, pickups) are string sprites designed at 8-16 px, drawn at scale 2 if they are the player's ship. Keep hitboxes smaller than the art.\n\n**Feel numbers** (world units per second, tile = 16): walk 70-90, run 95-140, jump 250-320 with gravity 800, fall cap 330, dash 200-260, bullets 250-320 (player) and 70-120 (enemy), enemy walk 25-45. Hit feedback: `game.hitFx` (Effects).\n\n## More: animation and examples outside this card\nThe repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; any file at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`) holds more to take from. Its files are large: search them and take only what the game needs. Real 3D (`E.World3D`: first person, racing, flight) is there: `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`.\n- **Motion clips**: 325 curated and 2,548 motion-capture takes that play on any Humanoid: find one in `src/mocap/catalogs/`, cut a small set with `tools/anim-set.mjs`, then `Mocap.drive(rig, lib)` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Where to find more; `docs/MOCAP.md`).\n- **Objects and materials** (full engine): `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a still object from any view and facing, `map.solid(...)` makes it solid; `E.MATERIALS` dresses floors (`floorMat`), walls (`face`, `roof`) and platform tiles (`mat`). `docs/ASSETS.md` is the card: the format, the library, writing one and checking it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`.\n- **Worked examples**: Emberdeep (`src/emberdeep/`), a whole game on this engine: 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies, 3 heroes and 30 skills, registered with `def(kind, id, spec)` (one search lists them); adapt them, they lean on the game's own helpers. `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body.\n- **Modules** (`engine/modules/<name>.js`; `modules/` in the handoff package): general systems in files of their own, each a `<script>` after the engine, its header its manual (read it there, or its section of the index). A kit carries the modules its slice names (`@modules` in its header); the full engine (`dist/my-3d2dge.js`; `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) carries them all.\n  - `grid`: square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges over heights and past allies, fields of view, `E.Fog`, groups, shapes of effect, threat maps, `E.GridMover`, `E.Turns` (speed, charge time, phases, ATB). ROGUELIKE, PUZZLE, TACTICS, ARCADE, ARTILLERY, RTS, SIM, 3D CRAWLER.\n  - `gen`: generated maps from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a TileMap reads. ROGUELIKE, ARCADE.\n  - `fight` (the full engine only): one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter` (frame data, hurt boxes from the rig's joints, guards, throws, cancels, juggles, specials by motion or charge, a meter, projectiles), `E.Match` (rounds, KO, the HUD, training), `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`. FIGHTING.\n  - `rooms`: a world of rooms on a grid of screens (`E.World`): walking between them at open edges, the explored map (`drawMap`, or `shellMap()` for `E.Shell`), gates that abilities or keys open, saves in `E.store`. METROIDVANIA.\n  - `mode7`: the SNES's Mode 7 (`E.Mode7`): a camera over a flat floor (a TileMap's, a function's or a canvas), a turning sky, billboards far to near (rigs, sprites, shapes, your drawing), fog, `project` and `toFloor`, split screens. KART.\n  - `road`: the pseudo-3D road (`E.Road`): segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance and hidden past a crest, the sky's parallax on curves. ROAD.\n  - `drive`: cars on the ground plane (`E.Car`: grip and slide, a kart's hop and drift boost, floor tags, walls and cars it bounces off) and races (`E.Race`: checkpoints along an `E.Path`, laps, places, drivers that pass and rubber-band, items, the HUD; the 3D engine's `world.vehicle` too). KART.\n  - `story`: dialogue as data (`E.Story`): lines with speakers and portraits, choices with conditions, flags and values, gotos, the game's events, a quest log, balloons over heads, a backlog, auto and skip; its state for a save. RPG, POINT-AND-CLICK, TACTICS.\n  - `rpg`: a role-playing game's rules (`E.RPG`): items, bags (slots or a grid of sizes), members with stats, levels and gear, status effects, a party (items used, rewards shared), shops, damage, random encounters by zone, list and card windows. RPG, 3D HORROR.\n  - `strategy` (the full engine only; needs `grid`): `E.Strategy` for real-time strategy (selection by click, box and group; queued orders; formations; production, resources, placement; fog, the minimap; an AI's build order and waves) and for sims `E.Clock` (days, the light), `E.needs` and `E.choose`, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`. RTS, SIM.\n  - `click` (the full engine only): point-and-click adventures (`E.Click`): painted rooms, walk areas with holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots with verbs, SCUMM's nine verbs, Sierra's icons, a verb coin or two buttons, an inventory, rules as data, exits, `story`'s talk; taps on a phone. POINT-AND-CLICK.\n  - `physics`: 2D rigid bodies (`E.Physics`): circles, boxes, polygons, segments, chains and arcs; bounce, friction, sensors, layers, sleep; fast balls swept, never passing through; the same world at any refresh rate; flippers, bumpers, slingshots, kickers. PINBALL, ARTILLERY.\n  - `terrain`: destructible ground in the side plane (`E.Terrain`): carved and added by circles and polygons, the ground below a point and its normal, rays for shots, walkers on slopes, a collider for `physics`. ARTILLERY.\n  - `songs`: the songbook (`E.Songbook`): songs by mood (field, town, battle, race, horror, chase, dance ...) and jingles (stage clear, game over, level up); `get(id)`, `find(mood)`. RHYTHM.\n  - `rhythm` (the full engine only): `E.Rhythm`: charts on a song's steps, judgments in ms of the music clock, combo, score, a life gauge, grades, holds, call and response, a note highway in any direction. RHYTHM.\n  - `arcade`: the coin-op's frame: scores that float up, extra lives, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode (a demo, the table, the title). ARCADE, SHOOTER, PINBALL.\n- **Everything running**: https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs\n\n## Before you hand the game back\n- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge` (between the markers in a single file; `game/game.js` in the handoff package). Only your game: no leftover starter menu or unused slices. No network, no libraries.\n- Title screen and game-over screen as scenes; `pausable: true` on gameplay scenes. No pause, settings, rebinding or full-screen code of your own (they're standard); prompts from `game.input.label(action)`.\n- The camera follows the player (`game.focus` every update) and shows the level; the HUD uses screen pixels, or `E.Shell`'s HTML for a modern game (the STEALTH kit; the handoff package's template).\n- Sound effects on jump, hit, pickup and death; music per scene.\n- The look target list above: backdrop, textured tiles, props, sized and dressed HD characters, effects, title font, lights. Compare your screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug, not with NES games.\n- If you can run commands: `node tools/check.mjs game/index.html` (your page; add `#scene` to jump to a scene). It presses start, plays, cycles the views, prints how much of the screen is filled and how many colors it uses, adds look notes (flat areas, checkerboard dithering, low contrast, a thin palette), and fails on any error. Act on every look note. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html#scene --steps \"wait:1500 press:Enter wait:800 rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records an animation frame by frame into one contact sheet: check every attack with it (a swing must wind up, strike, and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps).\n- Animation variety: every character has its own moves from `E.MOVES` (not one shared swing), enemies telegraph with a visible wind-up, and reactions play out (hurt, knockback, `down`, victory `cheer`).\n  /* =================================== 2. FORMATION =================================== */\n  const FORM = (() => {\n    const FW = 224, FLY = 130;   // the field's width (its height is the screen's: FH), a moth's speed along its path (px/s)\n    let FH = 270;\n    const MOTH = {\n      a: E.sprite(['..y..y..', '.yYyyYy.', 'ybbBBbby', 'bbBwwBbb', '.bBBBBb.', '..b..b..'], { y: '#ffe040', Y: '#fff8a0', b: '#4a8aff', B: '#8ab8ff', w: '#ffffff' }),\n      b: E.sprite(['r......r', '.rRRRRr.', 'rRwRRwRr', '.rRRRRr.', 'r.r..r.r'], { r: '#e03a4a', R: '#ff8a8a', w: '#ffffff' }),\n      boss: E.sprite(['..gGGg..', '.gGwwGg.', 'gGGGGGGg', 'gpGppGpg', '.p.pp.p.', 'p......p'], { g: '#3ad860', G: '#9af0a0', w: '#ffffff', p: '#c050f0' }),\n      hurt: E.sprite(['..pPPp..', '.pPwwPp.', 'pPPPPPPp', 'pgPggPgp', '.g.gg.g.', 'g......g'], { p: '#a050e0', P: '#d8a0ff', w: '#ffffff', g: '#3ad860' })\n    };\n    const SHIP = E.sprite(['...w...', '...w...', '..wrw..', '.wwrww.', 'wwbwbww', 'w.bbb.w', 'r..r..r'], { w: '#f0f0f8', r: '#ff4a4a', b: '#4a8aff' });\n    const ROWS = [['boss', 4], ['b', 8], ['b', 8], ['a', 10], ['a', 10]];\n    const SLOTS = ROWS.flatMap(([kind, n], row) => Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => ({ kind, x: FW / 2 + (i - (n - 1) / 2) * 18, y: 36 + row * 16 })));\n    let cab, demo, foes, shots, bombs, ship, wave, sway, dead, over, readyT, stars = [], X0 = 0, Y0 = 0;\n    // a wave: five groups of eight fly in from alternate sides, a loop on the way, each to its own place (Catmull paths)\n    function newWave() {\n      foes = []; wave++; cab.card('STAGE ' + wave, 1.8); readyT = Math.max(readyT, .6);\n      SLOTS.forEach((s, i) => {\n        const group = Math.floor(i / 8), side = group % 2 ? 1 : -1, k = i % 8;\n        const path = new E.Path([[FW / 2 + side * 150, -20], [FW / 2 + side * 70, 80], [FW / 2 - side * 20, 170], [FW / 2 - side * 70, 120], [FW / 2 - side * 30, 70], [s.x, s.y]], { smooth: 'catmull' });\n        foes.push({ kind: s.kind, hp: s.kind === 'boss' ? 2 : 1, slot: s, path, d: -(group * 1.6 + k * .14) * FLY, state: 'in', x: path.at(0).x, y: -20, a: Math.PI / 2 });\n      });\n    }\n    function begin(c, isDemo) { cab = c; demo = !!isDemo; wave = 0; over = false; shots = []; bombs = []; sway = 0; dead = 0; readyT = 1.4; ship = { x: FW / 2, y: FH - 28, cool: 0 }; stars = Array.from({ length: 90 }, (_, i) => ({ x: (i * 97) % FW, y: (i * 53) % 600, s: 20 + (i % 3) * 25 })); layout(); newWave(); }\n    // a dive: from its place a swoop out, at the ship (where it is now) and off the bottom; it comes back from the top\n    function dive(f) {\n      const sx = ship.x, out = f.x < FW / 2 ? -1 : 1;\n      f.path = new E.Path([[f.x, f.y], [f.x + out * 30, f.y - 16], [f.x + out * 46, f.y + 20], [sx + (Math.random() - .5) * 50, FH * .62], [sx + out * 40, FH + 24]], { smooth: 'catmull' }); f.d = 0; f.state = 'dive';\n    }\n    const kill = f => { f.hp = 0; cab.add(f.kind === 'boss' ? (f.state === 'dive' ? 400 : 150) : f.kind === 'b' ? (f.state === 'dive' ? 160 : 80) : (f.state === 'dive' ? 100 : 50), { x: X0 + f.x, y: Y0 + f.y }); P.sparks(X0 + f.x, Y0 + f.y, 4, 10); P.bits(X0 + f.x, Y0 + f.y, 4, 6, ['#ffe040', '#4a8aff', '#ff4a4a']); A.sfx('explode', { vol: .45 }); };\n    function step(dt, I) {\n      layout(); sway += dt; ship.y = FH - 28;\n      for (const s of stars) s.y = (s.y + s.s * dt) % 600;\n      if (readyT > 0) readyT -= dt;\n      if (dead > 0 && (dead -= dt) <= 0) { const was = cab.p, r = cab.die(); if (r === 'over') { over = true; return; } if (r === 'next') { was.save = { wave, foes }; const s = cab.p.save; if (s) { wave = s.wave; foes = s.foes; for (const f of foes) if (f.state !== 'form') { f.state = 'back'; f.y = -20; } } else { wave = 0; newWave(); } } ship.x = FW / 2; bombs.length = 0; readyT = 1.4; }\n      const live = dead <= 0 && readyT <= 0;\n      if (live) {   // the ship: left and right; two shots on screen at most\n        const mv = demo ? botMove() : I.move()[0]; ship.x = clamp(ship.x + mv * 115 * dt, 10, FW - 10); ship.cool -= dt;\n        if ((demo ? botFire() : I.down('fire')) && ship.cool <= 0 && shots.length < 2) { shots.push({ x: ship.x, y: ship.y - 8 }); ship.cool = .2; A.sfx('shoot', { vol: .35 }); }\n      }\n      for (const s of shots) s.y -= 270 * dt; shots = shots.filter(s => s.y > -10);\n      const sx = Math.sin(sway * 1.2) * 12;\n      let formed = 0;\n      for (const f of foes) {\n        if (f.state === 'in' || f.state === 'dive') {\n          f.d += FLY * (1 + wave * .04) * dt; if (f.d < 0) continue;\n          const p = f.path.at(f.d), u = f.state === 'in' ? Math.min(1, f.d / f.path.length) : 0; f.x = p.x + sx * u * u; f.y = p.y; f.a = p.angle;\n          if (f.d >= f.path.length) { if (f.state === 'dive') { f.state = 'back'; f.y = -20; f.x = f.slot.x + sx; } else f.state = 'form'; }\n          if (f.state === 'dive' && f.y < ship.y - 50 && Math.random() < dt * (.7 + wave * .1)) bombs.push({ x: f.x, y: f.y, vx: (ship.x - f.x) * .35, vy: 125 });\n        } else if (f.state === 'back') { f.y += 90 * dt; f.x += (f.slot.x + sx - f.x) * Math.min(1, dt * 4); f.a = Math.PI / 2; if (f.y >= f.slot.y) f.state = 'form'; }\n        else { f.x = f.slot.x + sx; f.y = f.slot.y + Math.sin(sway * 3 + f.slot.x * .1) * 1.5; f.a = Math.PI / 2; formed++; }\n      }\n      if (live && formed > 3 && Math.random() < dt * (.45 + wave * .15)) { const c = foes.filter(f => f.state === 'form' && f.hp > 0); if (c.length) { const f = c[Math.floor(Math.random() * c.length)]; dive(f); if (f.kind === 'boss') for (const e of c.filter(e => e !== f && e.kind === 'b' && Math.abs(e.slot.x - f.slot.x) < 20).slice(0, 2)) dive(e); A.sfx('whoosh', { vol: .3 }); } }\n      for (const b of bombs) { b.x += b.vx * dt; b.y += b.vy * dt; } bombs = bombs.filter(b => b.y < FH + 10);\n      for (const s of shots) for (const f of foes) if (!s.hit && f.hp > 0 && f.d >= 0 && Math.abs(s.x - f.x) < 7 && Math.abs(s.y - f.y) < 6) { s.hit = true; if (--f.hp > 0) { A.sfx('bump'); P.sparks(X0 + f.x, Y0 + f.y, 4, 4); } else kill(f); }\n      shots = shots.filter(s => !s.hit); foes = foes.filter(f => f.hp > 0);\n      if (live) for (const o of [...bombs, ...foes]) if (Math.abs(o.x - ship.x) < 6 && Math.abs(o.y - ship.y) < 6) { dead = 1.8; P.explosion(X0 + ship.x, Y0 + ship.y, 4, .9, { sound: true, flash: false, shake: 2, freeze: false }); if (o.hp) kill(o); break; }\n      if (!foes.length && dead <= 0) newWave();\n    }\n    // the demo's pilot: of a step left, none and a step right, the first where no bomb or diving moth will be in the next\n    // half second (a bomb's way read ahead to the ship's row), preferring the way to the moth nearest above; firing when\n    // under one\n    const hitAt = (x, t) => bombs.some(b => { const k = (ship.y - b.y) / b.vy; return k > -.05 && k < t && Math.abs(b.x + b.vx * k - x) < 9; }) || foes.some(f => f.state === 'dive' && f.d >= 0 && Math.abs(f.y - ship.y) < 40 && Math.abs(f.x - x) < 12);\n    const botMove = () => {\n      const t = foes.find(f => f.state === 'dive' && f.y < ship.y - 50) || foes.filter(f => f.d >= 0).reduce((p, f) => !p || Math.abs(f.x - ship.x) < Math.abs(p.x - ship.x) ? f : p, null);\n      const want = t ? clamp((t.x - ship.x) / 10, -1, 1) : 0, ways = [want, 0, -1, 1].map(m => Math.round(m)), q = m => clamp(ship.x + m * 115 * .3, 10, FW - 10);\n      for (const m of ways) if (!hitAt(q(m), .8) && !hitAt((ship.x + q(m)) / 2, .4)) return m === Math.round(want) ? want : m;\n      return ship.x < FW / 2 ? 1 : -1;\n    };\n    const botFire = () => foes.some(f => f.d >= 0 && Math.abs(f.x - ship.x) < 8);\n    function layout() { FH = Math.max(270, game.H - 4); X0 = Math.round((game.W - FW) / 2); Y0 = Math.round((game.H - FH) / 2); }\n    function draw(r) {\n      layout(); const cl = classic(), x0 = X0, y0 = Y0;\n      if (cl) r.sky(['#000000', '#000000']); else {\n        SPACE[0].draw(r);\n        r.decal(g => {   // a ringed gas giant low in the field, lit from the upper right: the ring's far half, the planet's banded\n          // shading (discs stepped toward the light), the ring's near half\n          const [ox, oy] = r.w(0, 0), cx = Math.round(ox + x0 + FW * .24), cy = Math.round(oy + y0 + FH - 40), R = 40;\n          const ring = near => { for (let a = 0; a < TAU; a += .012) { if ((Math.sin(a) > 0) !== near) continue; for (const [k, c] of [[1.55, '#8a6aa8'], [1.7, '#c8a8e0'], [1.85, '#6a4a88']]) px.dot(g, cx + Math.cos(a) * R * k, cy + Math.sin(a) * R * k * .22, c); } };\n          ring(false);\n          ['#1a1030', '#2a1a46', '#3e2860', '#563a7a', '#704e94', '#8c66ac', '#a882c2'].forEach((c, k) => px.disc(g, cx + k * 1.4, cy - k * 1.4, R - k * 4.5, c));\n          for (let b = -2; b <= 2; b++) { const y = b * 12 + 3, hw = Math.sqrt(Math.max(0, (R - 2) ** 2 - y * y)) - 3; px.blend(g, .16, 'normal', () => px.rect(g, cx - hw + 1, cy + y, hw * 2, 2, b % 2 ? '#2a1640' : '#e8c8a0')); }\n          ring(true);\n        });\n      }\n      r.overlay(g => {\n        for (const s of stars) if (s.y < FH) px.dot(g, x0 + s.x, y0 + s.y, s.s > 50 ? '#ffffff' : s.s > 30 ? '#8a8ab0' : '#4a4a6a');\n        for (const s of shots) { if (!cl) px.blend(g, .5, 'add', () => px.rect(g, x0 + s.x - 1, y0 + s.y - 1, 3, 7, '#8ad8ff')); px.rect(g, x0 + s.x, y0 + s.y, 1, 5, '#ffffff'); }\n        for (const b of bombs) { if (!cl) px.blend(g, .4, 'add', () => px.disc(g, x0 + b.x, y0 + b.y, 3, '#ff8a4a')); px.rect(g, x0 + b.x - 1, y0 + b.y - 1, 2, 4, '#ff6a4a'); }\n      });\n      // each moth turned to the way it flies (r.sprite's rot; in its place, facing down); the classic turns in 16 steps\n      for (const f of foes) if (f.d >= 0 || f.state !== 'in') r.sprite(x0 + f.x, y0 + f.y, 0, f.kind === 'boss' && f.hp === 1 ? MOTH.hurt : MOTH[f.kind], { rot: f.a + Math.PI / 2, anchor: 'center', steps: cl ? 16 : 32, glow: cl ? 0 : .3 });\n      if (dead <= 0 && !over && (readyT <= 0 || Math.floor(readyT * 8) % 2)) r.sprite(x0 + ship.x, y0 + ship.y, 0, SHIP, { anchor: 'center', glow: cl ? 0 : .4 });\n    }\n    return {\n      id: 'formation', label: 'FORMATION', res: [232, 270], about: 'MOTHS FLY IN, FORM UP AND DIVE', hue: ['#4a8aff', '#ff4a4a', '#ffe040'], touch: ['fire'], extra: [20000, 70000], light: 0, fills: true,\n      begin, revive() { over = false; ship.x = FW / 2; bombs.length = 0; readyT = 1.4; }, force() { over = true; }, step, draw,\n      box: () => [X0, Y0, FW, FH], edges: () => [X0 - 2, X0 + FW + 2],\n      get over() { return over; }, get level() { return wave; },\n      state: () => ({ ship: +ship.x.toFixed(2), foes: foes.length, wave, shots: shots.length, bombs: bombs.length })\n    };\n  })();\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat Attack-of-the-Timelout-Attack-of-the-Timelord-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022923/vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md && sed -n '1184,1245p' Attack-of-the-Timelout-Attack-of-the-Timelord-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022923/vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat Attack-of-the-Timelout-Attack-of-the-Timelord-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022923/vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/docs/ASSETS.md && sed -n '1184,1245p' Attack-of-the-Timelout-Attack-of-the-Timelord-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022923/vendor/engine-repo/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Objects and materials as data\n\nThe full engine draws still objects (barrels, tables, trees, wells) from a few lines of data, in every view and facing,\nwith the house shading and outline, and gives every surface (floors, wall faces, wall tops, platform tiles, the objects'\nown faces) a material from one open registry. An object costs one to a few hundred tokens of data (the core's median is\n125); you check it headless with one command. Read this card whole before you write one. (The agent edition keeps the\nregistries and ignores the calls: `E.addAssets` returns empty lists, the others null.)\n\n## Use one\n\n```js\n// in draw(r): feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor; its front toward facing\nr.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing: Math.PI / 2 });\n// once, before the updates: bodies stop at it, a body coming down lands on it, one wholly below passes under\nmap.solid('table', 120, 80, 0, { facing: 0 });\n// a sprite of it, for an inventory or a HUD (r.sprite, px.sprite)\nconst icon = E.shapeSprite('chest', { view: 'iso' });\n```\n\n- **r.shape options:** facing (radians, where the front points: 0 east, π/2 south, as written), scale, mats ({ slot:\n  material }), flip (mirrors it x to -x, collision too), params (a generator's: { h: 30 }), bias, hides (default true),\n  light (adds its lights to game.lights), shadow (default true).\n- **map.solid** takes the same id and placement, so one object can feed both calls. Bodies span z to z + h (E.Body's h, 32).\n- **In 3D** the 3D engine draws the same entries, materials and boxes: `world.shape(id, x, y, z, o)` (its header).\n- **Steep views** (top-down) draw shapes from the characters' lower angle, as rigs are drawn. The light comes from the\n  viewer's upper left in every view.\n- **Glow and fire:** a material with glow: 1 ignores the light and stays bright in the dark; an entry's flames ([[x, y, z,\n  w, h]]) draw the engine's animated flame (r.shape only: a sprite has none). Anything else that moves stays a prop\n  (r.prop) or your own code.\n- **Dressing a generated level:** a scene is a few entries as [id, across, forward] from an anchor (a wall spot, forward\n  into the room); place it whole or not at all, each part on free floor, after the level's own random picks and from a\n  seeded stream of its own, so the level stays as its seed builds it (Emberdeep's `src/emberdeep/53-scenes.js`).\n\n## The core library\n\n**Shapes** (sized for a 26-unit hero; generators take params): barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed,\nbookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, well, sign, lamppost, anvil; fence { len: 16 }, pine { h: 41, tiers: 4 },\ntree { h: 40, seed: 1 }, bush { r: 7 }, grass { h: 6, n: 6 }, rock { r: 7.5, seed: 2 }, crystal { h: 18, seed: 1 }.\n`r.shape('pine', x, y, 0, { params: { h: 30 } })`; `E.SHAPES.barrel.about` says what each is.\n\n**Materials:** woods oak walnut birch crate plank planks wood; stones stone cobble marble granite sandstone slate rock\nbrick flagstone ruin; metals iron steel gold copper bronze metal rusted; clay glazed porcelain; cloth linen burlap wool velvet leather;\nplants bark needles leaves shrub moss straw hedge grass; ground dirt sand snow mud; water ice crystal flame bone shingle;\nwalls timber plaster; tops speckle plain tiles slab; tiles block bonus ground pipe checker.\n\n## Materials on surfaces\n\n```js\nE.MATERIALS.mossy = { color: '#6a7a5a', pattern: 'stones', size: 6 };   // or the shorthand '#6a7a5a stones size=6'\nconst types = { 1: { h: 32, top: '#6a6280', side: '#4a4360', face: 'cobble', roof: 'slate' } };   // TileMap walls\nconst map = new E.TileMap({ rows, legend, types, floorMat: 'flagstone', floorMats: { water: 'water' } });   // floors\nconst level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows, legend, types: { 1: { kind: 'solid', style: 'block', mat: 'mossy' } } });\n```\n\nfloorMats is keyed by the floor tags of the legend ('~': { floor: 'water' }); either stands in for floorTex.\n\nA material is `{ color, pattern, ...the pattern's numbers, ramp, glow, over }`. Its five tones come from `E.tones(color)`\n(or its own `ramp` of five, dark to light); the light picks a band, the pattern moves it a step, so a material works on\nevery surface and in every palette and recolors with one hex. An unknown name warns with the list of names.\nOverlays, the last on top, each in its own color's tones: `over: [['moss', '#5f7a3a', { up: .6 }], ['cracks']]`; moss on\nwhat looks up, cracks (the material a step darker), dirt near the ground (h 6), rust; each takes amount and size.\n\n| Pattern | Numbers | What |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| none | | the light alone |\n| planks | bw 3, len 14, dir | boards with joints; staves on round parts; dir: 'v' turns them |\n| crate | frame 2, bw 3 | a framed, braced side; planks on its lid |\n| bricks | bw 6, bh 3, mortar | running bond |\n| stones | size 5 | irregular stones (Voronoi), dark joints, a lit rim |\n| tiles | size 4 | square tiles, grout |\n| speck, noise | amount .08; scale 4 | specks; soft patches |\n| foliage | size 3.5 | leaf clumps lit on top |\n| bark | | fissures; rings on a cut end |\n| metal | rivet | a shine where lit; rivets |\n| cloth | fold .9 | weave and folds |\n| glaze | | a glossy shine |\n| thatch, shingles | course; bw | straw courses; roof tiles |\n\nYour own pattern: `E.PATTERNS.waves = (s, m) => Math.sin(s.u / (m.size || 3)) > .8 ? 1 : 0` (s.u, s.v: world units\nacross and up the surface; s.lid, s.curved, s.lit, s.W, s.H; return a step, -1 to +2). A material may also paint one kind\nof surface its own way, per pixel: `face(wx, z, tones, type, cx, cy, fi, nh, h)` or `roof(wx, wy, tones, type)` returning\na color or null, `floor(x, y, tag, view)` returning [r, g, b].\n\n## Write one\n\nUnits: 16 = 1 m = one tile; x east, y south, z up. The origin is the middle of the footprint on the ground; write it\nfacing south (+y). Use whole or half units: a pixel at game size is about 0.7 units.\n\n```js\nE.SHAPES.stool = { about: 'round three-legged stool', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { w: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['cyl','w',[0,0,8],5,1.5], ['ring',3,3,['box','l',[0,0,0],[1.5,1.5,8],{rot:[0,-8,0]}]]] };\n```\n\nAn entry: `about`, `tags`, `mats` (slot to material; a slot name that is not in mats is a material itself), and `parts`, or\n`make(params)` returning parts with `params` its defaults. Optional: `scale` (times the placement's), `foot: [x0, y0,\nx1, y1]` (the footprint for collision, and for sorting when it stands raised; the bounds by default), `lights: [[x, y,\nz, color, radius]]`, `flames: [[x, y, z, w, h]]`.\n\n| Part | Form | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| box | `['box', m, at, [w, d, h], { rot }]` | at: the bottom middle |\n| cyl | `['cyl', m, at, r, h, { r2, seg, sq }]` | r2: top radius (0: a cone); sq squashes y; seg: 6 for a hexagon |\n| lathe | `['lathe', m, at, [[r, z], ...], { seg }]` | a profile turned about z, bottom to top; [0, z] closes an end |\n| ball | `['ball', m, at, r, { sz }]` | at: its centre; sz below 1 flattens it |\n| prism | `['prism', m, at, [[a, b], ...], h, { axis }]` | an outline pushed h: up z (outline x, y), or centred along y (outline x, z) or x (outline y, z) |\n| wedge | `['wedge', m, at, [w, d, h]]` | h tall at the back, down to the floor at the front |\n| rock | `['rock', m, at, r, { sz, seed, rough }]` | a lumpy, faceted ball; below the floor is not drawn |\n| use | `['use', id, at, { rot, scale, mats, ...params }]` | another shape inside this one |\n| mirror | `['mirror', 'x' \\| 'y' \\| 'xy', part]` | the part and its mirror copies |\n| ring, row | `['ring', n, radius, part]`, `['row', n, [dx, dy, dz], part]` | n copies round z (the part moved out to radius); n copies in a line, centred |\n| group | `['group', at, rot, [parts]]` | parts moved and turned together |\n\nEvery part takes `{ rot }`: a yaw in degrees, or [x, y, z] degrees. Write arrays densely (`[0,0,8]`, not `[0, 0, 8]`):\nspaces cost a third more tokens.\n\n### Worked entries\n\n```js\nbarrel: { about: 'oak barrel, two iron hoops', tags: 'container wood', mats: { w: 'oak', h: 'iron' },\n  parts: [['lathe','w',[0,0,0],[[0,0],[5,0],[6,3],[6.5,8],[6,13],[5,16],[0,16]]], ['row',2,[0,0,9],['cyl','h',[0,0,7.5],6.5,1.5]]] },\ncrate: { about: 'wooden crate, framed and braced', tags: 'container wood', mats: { c: 'crate' }, parts: [['box','c',[0,0,0],[15,15,14]]] },\ntable: { about: 'plank table, four legs', tags: 'furniture tavern', mats: { t: 'oak', l: 'walnut' },\n  parts: [['box','t',[0,0,10],[24,14,2]], ['mirror','xy',['box','l',[10,5,0],[2,2,10]]]] },\nchest: { about: 'oak chest, round lid, iron bands', tags: 'container treasure', mats: { w: 'oak', b: 'iron', l: 'gold' },\n  parts: [['box','w',[0,0,0],[16,10,8]], ['prism','w',[0,0,8],[[-5,0],[5,0],[4,3],[0,5],[-4,3]],16,{axis:'x'}],\n    ['mirror','x',['box','b',[5.5,0,0],[1.5,11,8.5]]], ['box','l',[0,5,5],[3,1,3]]] },\nlamppost: { about: 'iron street lamp, glowing head', tags: 'town light', mats: { m: 'iron', g: 'flame' }, lights: [[0,0,40,'#ffd070',70]],\n  parts: [['cyl','m',[0,0,0],2.5,3], ['cyl','m',[0,0,3],1,34], ['box','g',[0,0,37],[4,4,5]], ['cyl','m',[0,0,42],3.5,3,{r2:0,seg:4}]] },\nfence: { about: 'rail fence, len long (16)', tags: 'village farm', mats: { w: 'oak' }, params: { len: 16 },\n  make: p => [['mirror','x',['box','w',[p.len / 2 - 1,0,0],[2,2,12]]], ['row',2,[0,0,5],['box','w',[0,0,6],[p.len - 2,1,1.5]]]] },\n```\n\nA generator seeds its noise from its params (`E.rng(seed)`), never `Math.random`, so it draws the same pixels everywhere.\n\n## Make one: the recipe\n\n1. **Read this card whole.**\n2. **Find** the nearest entry: `node tools/asset-find.mjs \"tavern table\"` searches the core library and the store's sets\n   (`src/assets/sets/`, about a thousand objects, materials and sprites converted from CC0 libraries, each with where it\n   came from) and `--show set:id` prints one; the Asset Lab (`/asset-lab`) shows them all.\n3. **Copy** it and change its names, parts and materials, or write a new one from the parts above. Give the cue a player\n   knows it by parts of its own (a skep's coils as stacked rings, an anvil's horn, a barrow's open tub dark inside), and\n   keep small parts plain ('none', 'glaze'): a busy pattern on a part a few pixels wide reads as speckle.\n4. **Look:** `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` (a file that sets `E.SHAPES.stool`, or a store set; a core id\n   alone works too: `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs barrel`). It draws `check-output/asset-sheet/stool.png`: every view and\n   four facings, beside the hero, on a light and a dark floor. It lints what reads badly: too small from above, a part\n   under a pixel, too few tones, low contrast with the floor, its tokens. Read the picture, fix, repeat.\n5. **Use** it: `E.SHAPES.stool = {...}` and `r.shape('stool', ...)`; or a set file\n   (`(window.ASSETS = window.ASSETS || {}).MINE = { set: 'MINE', format: 1, materials, shapes }`) and\n   `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS.MINE)`, which names its entries 'MINE:stool' so they never collide, and returns the names.\n   A store set loads the same way (its script, then `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`);\n   `node tools/asset-set.mjs <sets> --ids a,b --name mine --out mine.js` cuts the few a game uses, with what they need\n   (Emberdeep's set dressing loads `src/assets/sets/emberdeep.js`, cut so).\n\n## Limits\n\n- Still objects. Lids that open, swinging signs and held weapons are code (or props) for now.\n- A shape sorts by one point, as props do; a long one (a bed, a cart) may cross a body walking right beside it.\n- Collision is the footprint's box, turned with the shape, and the bounds' height: a table is solid under its top too.\n- Bigger than about 240 pixels on screen, a shape skips the actors' effects (flash, x-ray).\n## 21d. Shell\n\n- `new E.Shell(game, o = {}) → shell`: new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings', meters: .125, standard: true }): an HTML interface over the game (see the section's header)\n- `new E.Shell({ theme })`: CSS variables (ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font: a family, titleFont), and scale: the whole interface that many times (a number, or a function of the window) for a TV across a room; tv: true picks one by the window's width (14 px text at 875 px, up to 30 at 1,875)\n- `new E.Shell({ screens })`: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\n- `new E.Shell({ settings })`: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max, step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard ones)\n- `new E.Shell({ store, meters })`: store: where an older version saved them; meters: meters per world unit for distances\n- `new E.Shell({ standard })`: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the settings' groups)\n- `shell.back()` †: what cancel does: the screen's own back(), or close it (back to the screen under it; closing the pause screen resumes)\n- `shell.dispose()` †: take its elements away and let go of the game (game.dispose() does it)\n- `shell.hide()` †: close every screen (and resume, when the menu paused the game)\n- `shell.hud(st = {})` †: the HUD, from this step's state (call it every step; a piece left out is hidden, and only what changed is redrawn)\n- `shell.hud({ objective })` †: { step: '01 / 04', title, text, at: { x, y, z }, from: hero } (the distance in meters, and above or below)\n- `shell.hud({ status, place, tip })` †: status: 'UNSEEN' or { text, color }; place: 'The Old Harbour'; tip: 'text' or { title, text }\n- `shell.hud({ bars, counts })` †: bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'bar' | 'pips', color }] (health, stamina, a meter); counts: [[label, value]]\n- `shell.hud({ prompt })` †: { key: 'E' (or act: 'interact': its key on the player's device), label, hint, progress: 0..1 }\n- `shell.hud({ keys, buttons })` †: keys: [['WASD', 'Move'], ...] (hidden on touch screens); buttons: [[label, fn or action]] (top right: pause and map where there is no Escape key)\n- `shell.hud({ minimap })` †: { map, at (with facing), markers: [{ x, y, kind: 'dot' | 'diamond' | 'ring' | 'cross', color }], onClick(x, y) (the world point clicked), fog (an E.Fog: unseen cells black, seen ones dim), camera: true (the ground the screen shows) }\n- `shell.screen` †: the name of the open screen ('pause', 'settings', 'controls', ...), or null while the game plays\n- `shell.settings` †: the player's settings: game.settings (the standard ones and the game's own, by key)\n- `shell.show(name, spec = {})` †: open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (strings or functions), actions: [[label, fn] or { label, fn, act, primary, disabled }], back (fn for cancel), rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY', '#A heading'] (settings' rows, changed as on the settings screen) }. From another screen it stacks (back() returns); shown again, it updates (the new spec over the old). 'settings' takes its items\n- `shell.show('title')` †: { name, subtitle, meta: [...], footer }\n- `shell.show('help')` †: { keys: [[key, what]], sections: [[heading, text]] }\n- `shell.show('results')` †: { emblem, stats: [[value, label]], medals: [[label, earned]] }\n- `shell.show('map')` †: { map, at, markers (a list or a function), labels: [[text, x, y]], onPick(x, y) }\n- `shell.toast(title, text = '', seconds = 4)` †: a message that slides in at the top for a few seconds\n- `shell.tutorial(title, text = '', seconds = 15)` †: a tutorial card (bottom left, dismissable) for some seconds; tutorial(null) takes it away\n- `shell.root = null` †: game: the game it is over; o: its options; root: its DOM element (null in a headless game)\n\n## 22. Gpu lighting (webgpu)\n\n- `game.gpu.active(view)` †: true when it lights frames in this view (ready, enabled and a ground view)\n- `game.gpu.snapshot()` †: offscreen mode only: read the last frame back as a PNG data URL\n- `game.gpu.status()` †: 'loading' | 'on' | 'off' | 'unavailable'\n- `game.gpu.adapterInfo` †: the GPU's description from the browser (vendor, architecture), or null\n- `game.gpu.ambient` †: the unlit color, [r, g, b] 0..1\n- `game.gpu.bands = 6` †: how many light bands (fewer is more retro)\n- `game.gpu.enabled = true` †: false switches it off for now (and the canvas lighting with it, unless game.lights.enabled)\n- `game.gpu.failed = null` †: why it couldn't start, or null\n- `game.gpu.fallback = true` †: false keeps a frame it can't draw unlit (by default the canvas lighting stands in, game.lights.on)\n- `game.gpu.glow = .22` †: the strength of px.glow surfaces\n- `game.gpu.map = null` †: the TileMap it measures walls on\n- `game.gpu.offscreen = false` †: render into a texture instead of the page (automated tests; read it with snapshot())\n- `game.gpu.onStatus = null` †: called (gpu) when its status changes\n- `game.gpu.ready = false` †: true once the GPU is set up\n- `game.gpu.shadows = true` †: casters block light\n- `game.gpu.skipped` †: never queue more than two frames: a slow GPU drops frames instead of building up lag\n- `game.gpu.spill = 1.1` †: how far light spills past its radius\n- `game.gpu.wrap = .45` †: how far light wraps round wall corners, 0..1\n\n## 23. Sessions\n\n- `E.session` †: records a play session on any device and replays it exactly (this section's header says how)\n- `E.session.frame` †: the frames run since the page loaded\n- `E.session.record(on)` †: turn recording on or off for the next page loads (each load is a session of its own)\n- `E.session.recording` †: true while this page load is recorded\n- `E.session.replaying` †: true while it is a replay\n- `E.session.wanted()` †: true when recording is on for page loads (record(true))\n\n## Module arcade: the coin-op's frame: scores, high-score tables and initials, lives, credits, turns, cards, the attract mode (engine/modules/arcade.js)\n\n- `new E.Arcade(game, o = {}) → arcade`: new E.Arcade(game, { id, lives: 3, extra: [] (the scores that give a life) or every (a life each that many points), credits: null (free play) or the coins a game takes, idle: 20, demo: 30, scores: 6 (the attract mode's seconds), hud: true, colors: [label, number, highlight] })\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"The selected title is **Attack of the Timelout**. The source research confirms that interception and the four distinct weapon families are central, so those will drive the campaign. The engine revision is verified; simulation, networking, and synthesized audio are now being built in parallel.\n"}}
